uidelines | Mom Wiki | Fandom (https://mom-cbs.fandom.com/wiki/Categorization_Guidelines)\nciteturn27086search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; Recurring Cast members are Cast members who portray Characters that appear in multiple episodes but not enough episodes to be considered a Main Character. ... Episodic Cast members are Cast members who portray Characters that appear in only one episode in the entire Series.\n## Cast[]\n\nWith Cast pages, there are types of Cast and the Cast must be categorized into whichever category applies to them, *the Category: \"`Cast`\", is compulsory, because they are a part of the Cast. This category is where any Cast, big or small on Mom appears. This is a compulsory Category.\n\n### Main[]\n\nWith Cast members, there are seven main Categories that they can fit into, the rest is for most pages and are special. The seven main Categories are:\n\n  * *Cast\n  * Actresses\n  * Actors\n  * Main Cast\n  * Recurring Cast\n  * Minor Cast\n  * Episodic Cast\n\nHere is a little explanation and breakdown of those Categories:\n\n  * If a Cast member, is a Actress, they must be listed under \"`Actresses`\" category. If they are not, please do not list them under this category. Actresses are female Actors.\n  * If a Cast member is a Actors, they must be listed under \"`Actors`\" category. If they are not, please do not list them under this category.   * If a Cast member, is a part of the Episodic Cast, they must be listed under \"`Episodic Cast`\" category. If they are not, please do not list them under this category. Episodic Cast members are Cast members who portray Characters that appear in only one episode in the entire Series. Those Characters they portray should be listed under the Episode Characters category.\n\n### Seasonal[]\n\nOnce again. Next come the Season categories for every Cast. Seasonal categories aren't that hard to figure out, if a Cast appears on a certain Season, or multiple Seasons, they shall get added to that Category. And it goes as listed:\n\n  * Season 1 Cast\n  * Season 2 Cast\n  * Season 3 Cast\n  * Season 4 Cast\n  * Season 5 Cast\n\nEXPLANATION:\n\n  * If the Cast appears in Season 1, they must be listed under \"`Season 1 Cast`\" category. If they are not, please do not list them under this category.\n  * If the Cast appears in Season 2, they must be listed under \"`Season 2 Cast`\" category. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAI series with consistent characters. Episodic AI video production. AI Creative Lead (https://aicreativelead.com/series)\nciteturn27086search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; An AI series with consistent characters is episodic video produced with generative AI in which every recurring character keeps the same identity across episodes: face, body, age, wardrobe, voice and way of moving. ... Cast voices per character, dialogue with lipsync, music and mix, in every language the series needs. ... Story and cast first, then a character bible and a world bible, then reference-locked generation of every shot, a continuity review per episode, voices with lipsync, edit, music and master.\n\nSeries · Episodic · Character-consistent\n\n# AI series with consistent characters, episode after episode\n\nThe hard part of AI video is not one beautiful shot. It is the same face, the same body, the same wardrobe and the same voice in shot 400 as in shot 1. We build series on a character system, so every episode looks like the same show.\n\nImage: Miguel Cartujo episode 1 cover, AI-generated series by AI Creative Lead On air · Season One\n\nDefinition\n\n## What is an AI series with consistent characters?\n\nAn AI series with consistent characters is episodic video produced with generative AI in which every recurring character keeps the same identity across episodes: face, body, age, wardrobe, voice and way of moving. Consistency is engineered before the first shot, through character and world bibles, and enforced during production with reference systems, continuity checks and a single editorial owner. The result is a show that can grow to dozens of episodes without the cast drifting.\n\n6 recurring characters held across 3 episodes\n\n5 locations reused shot to shot\n\n<7 days: Season One produced by one Creative Lead\n\nDeliverables\n\n## What do you get when you commission an AI series?\n\n### Character bible\n\nFront, profile and three-quarter references, expressions, wardrobe, props, voice. The document the whole series is built on.\n\n### World bible\n\nLocations, lighting rules, colour, camera language, typography and sound signature.\n\n### Episodes\n\nWritten, storyboarded, cast, shot, voiced, scored and mastered. Vertical 9:16 for social, 16:9 for broadcast and platforms.\n\n### Versions\n\nNew episodes, new languages with native lipsync, cutdowns and trailers, without re-producing the show.\n\n### Continuity log\n\nEvery character and location tracked per episode, so season two starts where season one ended.\n\nCase · Original series · 2026\n\n## Miguel Cartujo, Season One\n\nMiguel Cartujo, Season One\n\nMiguel Cartujo is an original episodic series produced end to end with generative AI. Three episodes, one hero, a recurring cast of six and five locations that come back from chapter to chapter. \n### How long does it take?\n\nIt depends on episodes, formats and languages. Miguel Cartujo Season One, three episodes, was produced in under a week by one Creative Lead. The schedule for a commissioned season comes with the treatment.\n\n### What does it cost?\n\nSeries are quoted per season, on the number of episodes, formats and languages. Because the bibles are reused, the cost per episode falls as the series grows. You get a closed budget with the treatment.\n\nClients\n\n## Who makes AI series, and who commissions them?\n\nAI series are produced by AI production companies and studios that own a character system, not by a single tool. AI Creative Lead is one of them: an AI production company that writes, casts, shoots, voices, edits and masters episodic content with generative AI, from a Creative Lead in Málaga for clients worldwide. These are the clients who commission them.\n\n### Brands\n\nthat want a recurring character and a show, not a one-off spot.\n\n### Platforms and apps\n\nthat need episodic content at volume with a stable cast.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRelevance-aware Multi-context Contrastive Decoding (https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/WACV2026/supplemental/Kim_Relevance-aware_Multi-context_Contrastive_WACV_2026_supplemental.pdf)\nciteturn27086search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Non-RMCD Best ... Non-RMCD Best\nwith the highest Lens retrieval score as retrieval target doc-\numents. In the case where less than ﬁve Lens retrieval re-\nsults exist, we additionally retrieve target documents with\nCLIP scores identical to the same process in InfoSeek until\nobtaining ﬁve target documents without duplicates. Docu-\nments retrieved with CLIP score are appended to Lens re-\ntrieval results. Since the document summary does not exist\nin KB for Encyclopedic-VQA, an additional step of deter-\nmining passages in documents (i.e. paragraph) to retrieve is\nrequired. Among top-k documents having at least n pas-\nsages in total, a retrieval score between a sample and a\npassage is deﬁned with a BM25 similarity [50] between a\nquestion and the passage. Based on calculated BM25 sim-\nilarities, we retrieve n passages with the highest similarity,\nwhere the retrieval score scj between a sample and a context\ncj is set as a BM25 similarity.\nOK-VQA. For OK-VQA, we directly adopt the retrieval re-\nsults used in the baselines [36, 37] where RMCD is imple-\nSensitivity analysis. From Tab. 11 to 16, we report perfor-\nmances on Encyclopedic-VQA under different hyperparam-\neters. Overall, RMCD shows robust performance regardless\nof the choice of hyperparameter. Concretely, it consistently\noutperforms the second-best method ‘concat’, referred to as\n‘Non-RMCD Best’ in tables, regardless of the hyperparam-\neters. In terms of M and m controlling the maximum and\nminimum value of context weights, we found that larger\nvalues show better performance in general. Regarding ϑ1\nand ϑ2, which control softmax temperature, results show\nthat adopting ϑ1 = 1 or ϑ2 = 1 still outperforms the second-\nbest performance. Such a result shows that adopting ϑ1 or\nϑ2 is an optional choice for better performance, as setting a\nsoftmax temperature to 1 is equivalent to eliminating those\nhyperparameters. Finally, we observe that the choice of ω\nand ε have minimal impact on the ﬁnal performance.\nSelection guideliens. Here, we provide guidelines for hy-\nperparameter selection, although RMCD is generally robust\nto hyperparameters. For reﬂection strength M, smaller val-\nues are preferable under poor retrieval, thus we adopt rela-\ntively low M = 3.0 for OK-VQA with noisy web corpus.\nSetting m to overly small values (below →1.5) leads to sub-\noptimal performance because it penalizes relevant contexts.\nFor ω and ε, performance remains stable across the ranges\nreported in Tab.13 and Tab.14. Softmax temperatures above\n1.0 work well for ω1, while values below 1.0 are better for\nω2.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChanging the way we think about character change in episodic television series (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/280034539_Changing_the_way_we_think_about_character_change_in_episodic_television_series)\nciteturn27086search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # Changing the way we think about character change in episodic television series ... In television series, the plot focuses on episodic adventures, and the core cast of characters are seen as fairly rigid actants that facilitate those adventures.\n----------------------------------------------------------------